A||N||9||85ZainoGT||adding oil?||||1||
Z||000000||85ZainoGT||12-21-2001||11:40 AM||||granted you get oil changed every 3000 miles about.. but do you all add a little bit of oil every week or so?  im wondering because I had to add some oil yesterday and I still have 1000 more miles... jus wondering if I may have a leak?<P>------------------<BR>Paul Ventimiglia<BR>1987 GT all the options :)<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpicNEW3bsmall2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||12-21-2001||12:03 PM||||All cars are different.  Mine don't use any oil between the 3000 mile oil changes, but my cars are low milage 84K and one with 6K on the new engine.<P>I'd expect a high milage engine to use more oil.  You need to see if its burning oil or leaking oil.  Check an easily accessable spark plug for condition and stick a drip pan underneath for leaking.||||reg||1
Z||000002||sqoach||12-21-2001||12:28 PM||||This is kinda on topic. One of my friends asked me about changing oil. He wanted to know what would happen if he didn't change the oil regularly enough. I didn't know for sure, but know it's a good idea to change it around 3-4000 miles. What happens if you don't change it for say, twice that long? And this is on a new car.<P>To keep this exactly on topic, I had to add a bit of oil (maybe half a quart at most) to my 83K SE not too long ago. It is time for another oil change, though. <P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>                  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigsqoach2.gif">   <BR><B>86 SE V6 - daily driver</B>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||12-21-2001||12:41 PM||||Oil begins to degerate, and form acidic compounds wich, as you may guess, start to eat moving parts. The other bit is that old oil doesn't "behave" properly, and may allow moving parts to come in contact. Not a good thing. ||||reg||7
Z||000004||speed racer||12-21-2001||01:10 PM||||I was going to change my oil and I wanted to know how much oil a 2.8 V-6 motor uses.<BR>Kyle<p>[This message has been edited by speed racer (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||GTDude||12-21-2001||01:31 PM||||2.8 uses 4.5 qts<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||avengador1||12-21-2001||06:06 PM||||Speaking of oil changes, my 98 Acura CL recomends changing the oil every 7500 miles and my father's Mercedes recomends that you wait for the service light to come on. I think it took about 10000 miles. I think it has to do with the newer engines having tighter tolerances or the oils lasting longer. Of course if you do severe driving or drive in dusty conditions, they all recomend that you change your oil sooner. By the way I don't need to add oil to my Fiero with 159000 miles between changes. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by avengador1 (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Master_Sushi||12-22-2001||01:07 AM||||I don't let the oil go any longer then 4k miles in my fiero. I also use Mobil 1, this oil is the best! Use a quality filter also, its a good idea to change the filter with the oil also. If you use good oil you can run it out a bit longer. If you just using cheap dino oil you probably want to change it sooner.<P><BR>I have heard some Mercedes hold like 8qts of oil. The more you have the longer you can go without changing I guess. My fathers SAAB holds 2.6QTS of oil! Needless to say it gets changed like every 2500K miles.<P><BR>One of the best things you can do for you car is do regular oil changes. This is even more important if you drive hard like I do  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||12-22-2001||12:49 PM||||depending on the car....<P>For a new car, do what the recomended service intervals say or you could void the waranty. Don't second guess the care maker on that one. Make sure you keep the documentation of all the service. That documentation can come in very handy if there's a problem. (The first reaction of the car maker will usually be to blaim the owner even on cars with known internal oil handling problems.)<P>New cars have extended service intervals for a number of reasons. To complicated to cover in detail here. A large item is that current API standards are getting very tough. I think the API code for cars is up to SJ now. It was SE or SF I think when our Fiero's were made. ( <A HREF="http://www.api.org" TARGET=_blank>www.api.org</A>  you can learn anything you want to know about oil/lubricant/fuel from that site or ones they link to.)<P>The mercedes very likely uses an new type of sensor that actually monitors the condition of the oil. You can learn more about them at <A HREF="http://www.delphiauto.com/" TARGET=_blank>Delphi Automotive Systems</A><P>One of the big advantages of those sensors, besides saving you unneeded changing, is that the car also generates less waste oil that has to be recycled etc. This helps the environment out.<P>On an older motor it's always hard to say exactly when the oil is shot. The general rule has been to err on the side of caution and use the 3000mile change interval.<P>Everything in regular oil starts changing the the minute it's put in the motor, including the oil itself. Synthetics are more stable but they too will eventually crap out.<P>As for adding oil... the car is burning some or leaking some, likely both.<P>clean the motor and a leak will fairly quickly reveal just where it is.<P>If you're burning it, there's not a whole lot you can do about it short of serious internal work on the engine.<P>One thing to check for a leak is the drain plug.... carefully inspect and/or replace the seal on it every time you change the oil. (Metal seal should not be reused. nylon ones can be reused a few times if they aren't damaged.) They are cheap insurance. I also install magnetic drain plugs in all my motors. They help collect suspended metal before it get's into the oil pump.<P>Next would be valve covers.... they often leak slow enough that the oil that comes out cooks onto the head/exhaust wiout making an obvious mess.<P>Finally, to answer the original question... No, I don't add oil between changes. 155,000 plus mile motor on went on Mobil 1 a couple years ago at around 140,000<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-22-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000009||SLYGUY||12-22-2001||09:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by avengador1:<BR><B>Speaking of oil changes<BR> 159000 miles between changes.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>OMG!!! Some people neglect oil changes but you are the CHAMPION!! <P>Just kiddin' I know what you meant. <BR>On this topic though, my GT leaks a little oil. Most of it from the passenger side of the rear. I probably have one or two bad valve cover gaskets, as it loses 1/4 - 1/2 qrt between changes.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Sly<BR>||||reg||1
